Worked example (illustrative only — pattern matches an Exercise.Domain / Exercise.Domain.Tests layout):

Suppose a recommendation calculator incorrectly includes a rest day in weekly volume:

// Exercise.Domain/Recommendations/WeeklyVolumeCalculator.cs
public int CalculateWeeklyVolume(IEnumerable<WorkoutDay> days)
{
    return days.Sum(d => d.SetCount); // bug: includes rest days
}

Smallest safe fix — one guard clause, no signature change, no restructuring:

public int CalculateWeeklyVolume(IEnumerable<WorkoutDay> days)
{
    return days.Where(d => !d.IsRestDay).Sum(d => d.SetCount);
}

Test (xUnit, matching tests/Exercise.Domain.Tests/Recommendations):

[Fact]
public void CalculateWeeklyVolume_ExcludesRestDays()
{
    var days = new[]
    {
        new WorkoutDay { SetCount = 10, IsRestDay = false },
        new WorkoutDay { SetCount = 5,  IsRestDay = true  },
    };
    var result = new WeeklyVolumeCalculator().CalculateWeeklyVolume(days);
    Assert.Equal(10, result);
}

Verify: dotnet test tests/Exercise.Domain.Tests — the new test must fail against the pre-fix code (confirms it's a real regression test, not a tautology) and pass after.

